# 🐧 Linux Command Line Fundamentals

## Overview

As part of the TryHackMe Pre-Security learning path, I learned the fundamentals of using the Linux Command Line Interface (CLI).

The exercises involved navigating the Linux filesystem, finding and reading files, identifying the current user and operating system, checking disk usage, and exploring the `/etc` directory.

This was my first practical exposure to using the Linux terminal for system interaction and basic investigation.

**Platform:** TryHackMe  
**Learning Path:** Pre-Security  
**Topic:** Operating Systems / Linux CLI  
**Status:** Completed 

---

## What is the Linux CLI?

The Linux Command Line Interface (CLI) is a text-based way of interacting with a Linux operating system.

Instead of relying on a graphical interface, commands can be entered into the terminal to navigate the filesystem, inspect files, retrieve system information, and perform administrative tasks.

---

# Commands Practiced

| Command | What I learned |
|---|---|
| `pwd` | Displays the current working directory |
| `ls` | Lists the contents of the current directory |
| `ls -l` | Displays a detailed listing of files and directories |
| `cd` | Changes the current directory |
| `cd ..` | Moves up one directory level |
| `find` | Searches for files and directories |
| `cat` | Displays the contents of a file |
| `whoami` | Displays the currently logged-in user |
| `uname` | Displays the Linux kernel name |
| `uname -a` | Displays detailed kernel and system information |
| `df -h` | Displays filesystem disk usage in human-readable format |
